## What the experiment says as it runs: its steps, board and answer. These texts come with the experiment when it opens; its card is in contact-forces.ftl.

contact-forces-magnet-say = The magnet stands on the scales, zeroed. We lower a steel ball on a thread over it, to a gap of { $gap }. The ball does not touch the magnet. What will the scales show?
contact-forces-magnet-button = 🧲 Lower the ball
contact-forces-magnet-plan = ball over the magnet on the scales
contact-forces-magnet-title = magnet and ball across air
contact-forces-magnet-seen = The scales show { $reading }: the ball pulls the magnet up, the magnet pulls the ball down. { $force }, with no touching.
contact-forces-card-say = Now we slide a card between the magnet and the ball. The same gap, { $gap }.
contact-forces-card-button = 🧲 Slide in the card
contact-forces-card-plan = card between the magnet and the ball
contact-forces-card-title = magnet and ball through a card
contact-forces-card-seen = The scales show the same { $reading }: the card does not stop the field. { $force }.
contact-forces-comb-say = One bit of paper is too light for the scales, so we weigh a hundred together. Then we rub a plastic comb on hair and bring it to the bits on the table.
contact-forces-comb-button = 🪮 Comb and paper
contact-forces-comb-plan = rubbed comb over bits of paper
contact-forces-comb-title = comb and a bit of paper
contact-forces-comb-seen = A hundred bits come to { $reading }. The comb lifted a bit, so its force is more than the bit’s weight: { $force }.
contact-forces-table-say = We pull the block along the table with a dynamometer, steadily. It touches the table. What will the dynamometer show?
contact-forces-table-button = 🧱 Pull along the table
contact-forces-table-plan = block on the table
contact-forces-table-title = block on the table
contact-forces-table-seen = The dynamometer shows { $reading }: that is the block’s friction on the table.
contact-forces-lifted-say = We hang the same block on a thread, just clear of the table, and pull it aside. Is there any friction left?
contact-forces-lifted-button = 🧱 Block above the table
contact-forces-lifted-plan = block above the table
contact-forces-lifted-title = block above the table
contact-forces-lifted-seen = The block moves at the slightest pull, the dynamometer shows { $reading }. No touching, no friction.
contact-forces-fall-say = And last: the weight hangs on the dynamometer. We look at the reading and let go.
contact-forces-fall-button = ⬇ Let the weight go
contact-forces-fall-plan = weight on the dynamometer
contact-forces-fall-title = weight and the Earth
contact-forces-fall-seen = The dynamometer showed { $reading }: that is how hard the Earth pulls the weight. Let go, it fell, though the Earth does not touch it.
contact-forces-answer-why = Some forces act at a distance. A magnet attracts a steel ball across air and through a card, a rubbed comb a light bit of paper, and the Earth everything around it. Such bodies act on each other through a field: magnetic, electric, gravitational. The lines in the picture show the direction of the magnetic field: they leave the north pole and enter the south pole. They are neither waves nor a flow: a magnet at rest gives nothing off, and the lines themselves are imaginary.

    The scales showed one thing more: the force acts on both bodies. The ball pulls the magnet up just as the magnet pulls it down, which is why the magnet got «lighter». Friction and elastic force are contact forces: they appear only when bodies touch. A block lifted above the table feels no friction from it. The air passes nothing on here: a magnet attracts the ball in a vacuum too.
contact-forces-answer-say = Without touching, the magnet, the comb and the Earth acted; friction disappeared.
contact-forces-answer-text = Without touching: magnet and ball — { $magnet }, through the card — { $card }; comb and bit of paper — { $comb }; the Earth and the weight — { $fall }. The block’s friction: { $table } on the table, { $lifted } above it.
contact-forces-busy = Let’s see…
contact-forces-below-division = a force below the division of the scales
contact-forces-board-loss = Δm
contact-forces-board-friction = F = F friction
contact-forces-bad-gap = Closer than 1 cm the model fails: the ball is no longer small beside the distance.
contact-forces-bad-mass = The mass must be above zero.
contact-forces-scene-field = magnetic field lines
contact-forces-scene-scale = gap not to scale
contact-forces-aside-gilbert = In 1600 the English physician William Gilbert published his book On the Magnet. He made a model of the Earth, which he called a terrella, a “little Earth”, and saw that a compass needle behaves near it as it does on the Earth. His conclusion: the Earth itself is a great magnet, which is why the needle turns north without anything touching it. Gilbert also told electric effects from magnetic ones and named them after the Greek word for amber.
